# The timeline and playback

The editor window shows your project in four areas: the toolbar, the
preview, the inspector and the timeline. This page covers how to move around
the timeline and play the project back: tracks and their switches, the ruler
and playhead, zoom and row heights, selection, snapping, markers, the in/out
range, clip names and labels, and the View menu items for the timeline and
the preview. Editing with gestures (moving, trimming, slipping and so on) is
on [Editing](editing.md).

## The editor window

| Area | Where | What it holds |
|---|---|---|
| Title bar | top | The project name, with canvas size, frame rate and "edited" (while unsaved) as the subtitle, e.g. `1920 × 1080 · 30 fps · edited` |
| Toolbar | under the title bar | Transport, timecode, preview zoom, safe-area guides, **Export…** |
| Preview | middle left | The frame under the playhead, at canvas size, letterboxed |
| Inspector | middle right | The Clip, Media, Document, Transcript and Assistant tabs |
| Timeline bar | above the timeline | The selection block, clip buttons, Snap, the level meter, zoom, track size (see [below](#the-timeline-bar)) |
| Timeline | bottom | The ruler, track headers and lanes |

Drag the dividers to resize the panes. The inspector can be 280–440 pt wide
(304 pt to start) and the timeline 140 pt up to 60 % of the window (300 pt to
start). The sizes you drag to are remembered. Dragging the inspector divider
to the edge stops at its minimum width; the only way to hide the inspector is
**View ▸ Hide Inspector** (⌥⌘I), which becomes **Show Inspector** while it is
hidden and is also remembered. The window is at least 900 × 620 pt.

### The toolbar

From left to right:

- **Transport**: Go to Start (Home), Previous Edit Point (↑), Play / Pause
  (Space), Next Edit Point (↓), Go to End (End), and **Loop**. Loop is dimmed
  while off.
- **Timecode**: the playhead's time, then `/` and the duration of what the
  timeline shows. Click the time to type one: it takes `HH:MM:SS:FF`,
  `HH:MM:SS.mmm`, `HH:MM:SS`, `MM:SS`, seconds with a decimal (`83.5`) or a
  bare number of seconds. Press Return to jump there, snapped to a frame.
  Text that is not a time beeps and the field goes back to the playhead's
  time.
- **Preview zoom**: a popup with Fit, 50%, 100% and 200%.
- **Safe**: shows the title-safe and action-safe guides (90 % and 80 %
  rectangles) on the preview. Remembered between launches.
- **Export…** (⌘E): see [Export](export.md).

Timecode shows frames by default. The Document tab's **Timecode** popup
(Frames or Milliseconds) changes every readout, the ruler included.

### The timeline bar

From left to right:

- **The selection block**, naming what is selected (see
  [Selecting clips](#selecting-clips)).
- **Eight clip buttons**: Split (⌘T), Trim Start to Playhead (W), Trim End
  to Playhead (E), Ripple Delete (⌘⌫), Add Marker (M), Duplicate (⌘D), Add
  Text (T) and Add Shape. Each button's tooltip names its key. **Add Shape**
  opens a menu of shapes and has no menu-bar item, so this button is the way
  to add one. What the edits do is on [Editing](editing.md); text and shapes
  are on [Canvas and layers](canvas-and-layers.md).
- **Snap**, the snapping checkbox (see [Snapping](#snapping)).
- **The level meter** (see [The level meter](#the-level-meter)).
- **The zoom cluster**: −, the zoom slider, + and Fit (⇔) (see
  [Zooming the timeline](#zooming-the-timeline)).
- **S / M / L**, the track size (see [Row heights](#row-heights)).

## Tracks

There is one kind of track, and it holds any clip: video, audio, a still,
text, a shape, a graphic, a nest or an adjustment clip. Tracks stack: **the
top lane is the front-most**, and the bottom lane is the back.

### Track headers

Each track has a header on the left with its name and five switches:

| Switch | Symbol (off / on) | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| Hide Video | eye / struck-through eye | Takes the track's picture out of the preview and export. Its clips draw dimmed. |
| Mute Audio | speaker / crossed-out speaker | Silences the track's sound. |
| Lock Track | open padlock / closed padlock | Protects the track from edits: its clips can't be dragged or trimmed, a marquee skips them, and ripple, insert and range edits leave them alone. The lane is hatched. |
| Solo | headphones (both) | Silences every other track that is not soloed too. Pictures stay. Mute still wins on a track that is both muted and soloed. |
| Mix | sliders (both) | Opens the track's mix panel: volume, pan, compressor and limiter ([Audio](audio.md#track-volume-and-pan)). Lit while the volume or pan is not at its default, or a compressor or limiter is on. |

A lit switch is also highlighted in the accent colour, so you can read a
track's state from the symbol or the highlight. Under a solo, the waveforms
on the silenced tracks draw faded but their speakers stay unlit, because the
speaker shows the track's own setting.

A track with no name shows `Track N`, counting up from the back track.

### Managing tracks

| To | Do this |
|---|---|
| Add a track at the front | **Track ▸ New Track** (⇧⌘N) |
| Rename a track | Double-click its name in the header |
| Reorder | Drag a header up or down. A coral line shows where it will land. |
| Select every clip on a track | ⌥-click its header, or **Edit ▸ Select All on Track** (the current track, below) |
| Remove a track | **Track ▸ Remove Track**, or the header's context menu |
| Drop every empty track | **Track ▸ Remove Empty Tracks** |

Dragging a clip above the top lane or below the bottom lane makes a new track
for it (see [Editing](editing.md)).

**Remove Track** removes an empty track at once. If the track still holds
clips it asks first ("The track still holds 3 clips, which will be removed
with it.") with **Remove** and **Cancel**. The last track can't be removed.
**Remove Empty Tracks** always keeps at least one track.

The **Track** menu works on the *current track*: the first selected clip's
track, or the front-most track when nothing is selected. Besides the commands
above it has **Hide Video**, **Mute Audio**, **Lock Track** and **Solo**,
which flip that track's switches, and **Mix…**, which opens the
track's mix panel. Only Solo shows a checkmark there. The
**Matte ▸** submenu is described in
[Canvas and layers](canvas-and-layers.md).

### The track context menu

Right-click (or ⌃-click) a header, or an empty part of a lane, to get that
track's menu: **Hide Video**, **Mute Audio**, **Lock Track** and **Solo**,
each checked when on, then **Mix…**, then **Matte ▸**, then
**Remove Track** (disabled on the last track). Right-clicking a clip opens no menu.

Every switch flip, rename and reorder is one undo step. The names **Edit ▸
Undo** shows for this page's edits are:

| Edit | Undo name |
---
// The facts a reader wants before the prose: where a feature is, its keys, what it needs.
// Every prop is optional, and a row appears only when its prop is given.
interface Props {
  /** Menu paths, as the app prints them: `Clip ▸ Split`. */
  menu?: string | string[];
  /** Keys, as the app prints them: `⌘T`. */
  keys?: string | string[];
  /** Where else it lives: an inspector tab and section, the toolbar, a right-click menu. */
  where?: string | string[];
  /** The agent tools that do the same thing. */
  tools?: string | string[];
  /** What it needs beyond the app: a key, a permission, a model. */
  needs?: string | string[];
}

const list = (value?: string | string[]) => (value === undefined ? [] : [value].flat());
const { menu, keys, where, tools, needs } = Astro.props;
---

<dl class="fj-glance not-content">
  {list(menu).length > 0 && (
    <>
      <dt>Menu</dt>
      <dd>
        {list(menu).map((item) => (
          <strong>{item}</strong>
        ))}
      </dd>
    </>
  )}
  {list(keys).length > 0 && (
    <>
      <dt>Keys</dt>
      <dd>
        {list(keys).map((item) => (
          <kbd>{item}</kbd>
        ))}
      </dd>
    </>
  )}
  {list(where).length > 0 && (
    <>
      <dt>Where</dt>
      <dd>
        {list(where).map((item) => (
          <span>{item}</span>
        ))}
      </dd>
    </>
  )}
  {list(needs).length > 0 && (
    <>
      <dt>Needs</dt>
      <dd>
        {list(needs).map((item) => (
          <span>{item}</span>
        ))}
      </dd>
    </>
  )}
  {list(tools).length > 0 && (
    <>
      <dt>Agent tools</dt>
      <dd>
        {list(tools).map((item) => (
          <code>{item}</code>
        ))}
      </dd>
    </>
  )}
</dl>
import AppKit

/// The document window. Nothing but sizing and the subtitle lives here — the
/// editor itself is `EditorViewController`.
final class EditorWindowController: NSWindowController {
    init(document: ProjectDocument) {
        let window = EditorWindow(
            contentRect: NSRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: 1280, height: 800),
            styleMask: [.titled, .closable, .miniaturizable, .resizable],
            backing: .buffered,
            defer: false)
        window.contentViewController = EditorViewController(document: document)
        window.backgroundColor = DS.chromeBackground
        window.titlebarAppearsTransparent = false
        window.minSize = NSSize(width: 900, height: 620)

        // Content size: the canvas at a comfortable preview scale plus the
        // chrome around it (toolbar 44, inspector 304, timeline 300), clamped
        // to 85 % of the screen. A 4K project must not open a 4K window.
        let canvas = document.project.snapshot.canvas
        var contentSize = NSSize(
            width: min(canvas.size.width, 1280) + EditorViewController.defaultInspectorWidth,
            height: min(canvas.size.height, 720) + EditorViewController.toolbarHeight
                + EditorViewController.defaultTimelineHeight)
        if let screen = NSScreen.main {
            let limit = screen.visibleFrame
            contentSize.width = min(contentSize.width, limit.width * 0.85)
            contentSize.height = min(contentSize.height, limit.height * 0.85)
        }
        contentSize.width = max(contentSize.width, 900)
        contentSize.height = max(contentSize.height, 620)
        window.setContentSize(contentSize)
        window.center()

        super.init(window: window)

        // The title tracks the document name through NSDocument's own
        // synchronizeWindowTitleWithDocumentName; only the subtitle is ours,
        // and the editor refreshes it whenever the canvas changes.
        window.subtitle = EditorWindowController.subtitle(for: document)
    }

    @available(*, unavailable)
    required init?(coder: NSCoder) {
        fatalError("EditorWindowController does not support NSCoder")
    }

    /// "1920 × 1080 · 30 fps · edited" — the document facts that are never in
    /// the title. The chrome cleanup made the title bar carry the project's
    /// identity (there is no status bar and the toolbar no longer shows
    /// document meta), so the dirty flag has to read out here in words: under
    /// `ProjectDocument.autosavesInPlace` AppKit never sets
    /// `NSWindow.isDocumentEdited`, so there is no close-button dot and this
    /// suffix is the only dirty indicator the window has. Static so
    /// `EditorViewController` can refresh it after a `set_canvas` without
    /// reaching into this controller's internals.
    ///
    /// **Both halves are published, and not from here.** The only caller that
    /// re-reads this is `EditorViewController.refreshFromDocument`, which runs
    /// off `.projectDocumentDidChange`; `ProjectDocument.publishingEditedState`
    /// posts that whenever `isDocumentEdited` crosses, so edited → clean lands
    /// after ⌘S and after every silent autosave-in-place, not just
    /// clean → edited. Do not try to move that hook here: overriding
    /// `NSWindowController.setDocumentEdited` is dead code, because — measured
    /// against an AppKit harness — a document whose `autosavesInPlace` is true
    /// never receives that message at all (with it false the override does get
    /// (false)/(true)/(false) on `addWindowController`/`.changeDone`/
    /// `.changeCleared`). It belongs in `ProjectDocument`, which owns the
    /// count, and it has to hook BOTH counting entry points: a save does not
    /// pass through `updateChangeCount(_:)` — an autosaving document clears
    /// through `updateChangeCount(withToken:for:)` instead.
    static func subtitle(for document: ProjectDocument) -> String {
        let canvas = document.project.snapshot.canvas
        let meta = "\(canvas.width) × \(canvas.height) · \(canvas.fps.displayName) fps"
        return document.isDocumentEdited ? meta + " · edited" : meta
    }
}
import AppKit

/// The editor's two split views, which know when a person is dragging a
/// divider (#327).
///
/// AppKit's divider drag runs inside `mouseDown(with:)` until the mouse is
/// up, so the flag is true for exactly the resizes a drag causes. The
/// notification's `NSSplitViewDividerIndex` user info is not that test:
/// NSSplitView.h says that since macOS 12 it is posted "during resize and
/// layout events as well", so a window resize carries it too.
final class PaneSplitView: NSSplitView {
    private(set) var isDraggingDivider = false

    override func mouseDown(with event: NSEvent) {
        isDraggingDivider = true
        defer { isDraggingDivider = false }
        super.mouseDown(with: event)
    }
}

extension EditorViewController {
    /// A drag of `main`'s divider saves `timeline`'s height under
    /// `timelineHeightKey`; of `upper`'s, `inspector`'s width under
    /// `inspectorWidthKey` (not while hidden, not ≤ 1 pt). Any other resize —
    /// `notification.object` not dragging, as a window resize, a restore or
    /// ⌥⌘I is — saves nothing, so the remembered size is the one a person
    /// chose and a new window opens with it.
    static func savePaneSize(
        after notification: Notification, main: PaneSplitView, upper: PaneSplitView,
        timeline: NSView, inspector: NSView, to defaults: UserDefaults
    ) {
        guard let split = notification.object as? PaneSplitView, split.isDraggingDivider
        else { return }
        if split === main, timeline.bounds.height > 1 {
            defaults.set(Double(timeline.bounds.height), forKey: timelineHeightKey)
        } else if split === upper, !inspector.isHidden, inspector.bounds.width > 1 {
            defaults.set(Double(inspector.bounds.width), forKey: inspectorWidthKey)
        }
    }
}
/// The three containers are the seams: a later stage puts
/// `PreviewCanvasView` in `canvasContainer`, `InspectorViewController`'s view
/// in `inspectorContainer` and `TimelineView` in `timelineContainer`, and
/// replaces the placeholders that stand in for them now.
final class EditorViewController: NSViewController {
    // MARK: - Metrics (spec §3's table)

    static let toolbarHeight = DS.toolbarHeight
    /// The redesign's inspector width (`DS.panelWidth`, 304). The two clamps
    /// below are the window's, not the design's, and stay where they were.
    static let defaultInspectorWidth = DS.panelWidth
    static let minInspectorWidth: CGFloat = 280
    static let maxInspectorWidth: CGFloat = 440
    /// 300, not the 240 that predates the redesign: lane heights are per KIND
    /// now (`DS.trackHeights`), so a Medium text/video/audio stack is
    /// 44 + 84 + 60 pt of lanes plus 2 pt between each, the 22 pt ruler, the
    /// timeline's own 36 pt bar and 4 pt of lane inset top and bottom — 260 pt
    /// before a fourth track, against a 240 pt pane that clipped the last one.
    /// The DEFAULT only: the two clamps below and the persisted
    /// `timelineHeightKey` are untouched, so a pane the user has already sized
    /// keeps the size they gave it.
    static let defaultTimelineHeight: CGFloat = 300
    static let minTimelineHeight: CGFloat = 140
    /// The timeline may not eat more than 60 % of the window (spec §3).
    static let maxTimelineFraction: CGFloat = 0.6
    static let minCanvasWidth: CGFloat = 380
    static let minUpperHeight: CGFloat = 220
    /// The void margin around the preview (spec §4.2).
    static let canvasMargin: CGFloat = 24

    // MARK: - Persisted view state

    /// Pane sizes and the toolbar toggles. View state, so `UserDefaults` and
    /// not the project (spec §5.2's last bullet).
    static let inspectorWidthKey = "FrameJobInspectorWidth"
    static let timelineHeightKey = "FrameJobTimelineHeight"

import AppKit

/// The 36 pt bar over the lanes (the redesign's §4): a fixed selection block
/// at the left edge that never moves, then the tools.
///
/// It mirrors Rasterize's options bar — identity first, then what acts on it.
/// Duplicate, Add Text and Add Shape came DOWN from the window toolbar in the
/// same change: they act on a clip, so they belong next to blade and ripple
/// delete rather than beside Export. Built here rather than in
/// `TimelineView.swift`, which is frozen at wiring (app/CLAUDE.md); the view
/// keeps only the three handles it later refreshes.
enum TimelineBar {
    /// What `TimelineView` has to hold on to after the bar is built: the
    /// block it pushes a selection into and the two option controls it asks
    /// to re-read themselves.
    ///
    /// The level meter is deliberately NOT here. It subscribes to
    /// `LevelMeter.shared` itself, so nothing has to push into it and the
    /// frozen `TimelineView` and `EditorViewController` gain neither a stored
    /// handle nor a fan-out line for it.
    struct Parts {
        let view: NSView
        let selection: TimelineSelectionBlock
        let snap: OptionCheckboxControl
        let size: OptionSegmentedControl
    }

    /// The design's 12 pt edge padding and 12 pt between clusters.
    private static let padding: CGFloat = 12
    /// Edit-action buttons: 28 × 24 at 17 pt, on the square button radius.
    private static let actionSize = NSSize(width: 28, height: 24)
    private static let actionPointSize: CGFloat = 17

    static func build(owner: TimelineView) -> Parts {
        let bar = BarView(border: .both)

        let selection = TimelineSelectionBlock()

        let actions = NSStackView(views: editActions())
        actions.orientation = .horizontal
        actions.spacing = 2

        let snap = OptionCheckboxControl(
            label: "Snap",
            read: { [weak owner] in owner?.snappingEnabled ?? false },
            write: { [weak owner] _ in owner?.onSnappingToggled?() },
            enabled: { true },
            onEdit: { [weak owner] in owner?.snapToggle?.refresh() })

        let size = OptionSegmentedControl(
            segments: [
                (symbol: "framejob.small", fallback: "S", tooltip: "Small tracks"),
                (symbol: "framejob.medium", fallback: "M", tooltip: "Medium tracks"),
                (symbol: "framejob.large", fallback: "L", tooltip: "Large tracks"),
            ],
            read: { [weak owner] in owner?.trackHeightIndex ?? 1 },
            write: { [weak owner] index in owner?.onTrackHeightChanged?(index) },
            segmentEnabled: nil, enabled: { true },
            onEdit: { [weak owner] in owner?.heightControl?.refresh() })

        let spacer = NSView()
        spacer.setContentHuggingPriority(NSLayoutConstraint.Priority(1), for: .horizontal)
        spacer.setContentCompressionResistancePriority(
            NSLayoutConstraint.Priority(1), for: .horizontal)

        // The meter goes in the flexible gap, immediately left of the zoom
        // cluster, rather than last: placing it after S/M/L would shift the
        // zoom cluster and the size control 130-odd points left, and both are
        // two phases of muscle memory old. Here nothing that shipped moves at
        // any window width, because the meter carries the spacer's own
        // priority-1 hugging and compression resistance and absorbs slack up
        // to its own cap.
        //
        // It gets NO divider before it, and that is arithmetic rather than
        // taste: the row's fixed content measures 880 pt against a 900 pt
        // minimum window, one more stack gap for the meter makes 892, and a
        // divider's second gap plus its hairline would make 905 — broken
        // constraints at the minimum window, which nothing in this project
        // asserts on and no proof would catch. The meter compresses to
        // nothing and stops drawing instead (`LevelMeterView.draw`).
        let row = NSStackView(views: [
            selection, DividerView(length: 18), actions, DividerView(length: 18), snap, spacer,
            LevelMeterView(), zoomCluster(owner: owner), DividerView(length: 18), size,
        ])
        row.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        row.orientation = .horizontal
        row.alignment = .centerY
        row.spacing = padding
        bar.addSubview(row)
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            row.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: bar.leadingAnchor, constant: padding),
            row.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: bar.trailingAnchor, constant: -padding),
            row.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: bar.centerYAnchor),
        ])
        return Parts(view: bar, selection: selection, snap: snap, size: size)
    }

    /// The eight actions, in the design's order. Every selector already exists
    /// on `EditorViewController` and every button dispatches through the
    /// responder chain, exactly as the old timeline toolbar did.
    ///
    /// The design lists seven; **Add Shape is the eighth and is deliberate**.
    /// Shape has no menu item anywhere in the app, so its toolbar button was
    /// the only way to reach the feature — dropping it with the rest of the
    /// window toolbar would have deleted a command, not moved one.
    private static func editActions() -> [NSView] {
        let items: [(String, String, String, Selector)] = [
            (
                "scissors", "✂", "Split (⌘T)",
                #selector(EditorViewController.splitAtPlayhead(_:))
            ),
            (
                "arrow.right.to.line", "⇥", "Trim Start to Playhead (W)",
                #selector(EditorViewController.trimStartToPlayhead(_:))
            ),
            (
                "arrow.left.to.line", "⇤", "Trim End to Playhead (E)",
                #selector(EditorViewController.trimEndToPlayhead(_:))
            ),
            (
                "delete.left", "⌫", "Ripple Delete (⌘⌫)",
                #selector(EditorViewController.rippleDeleteClips(_:))
            ),
            ("bookmark", "◆", "Add Marker (M)", #selector(EditorViewController.addMarker(_:))),
            (
                "doc.on.doc", "⧉", "Duplicate (⌘D)",
                #selector(EditorViewController.duplicateClips(_:))
            ),
            ("textformat", "Aa", "Add Text (T)", #selector(EditorViewController.addTextClip(_:))),
            (
                "square.on.circle", "▭", "Add Shape",
                #selector(EditorViewController.showAddShapeMenu(_:))
            ),
        ]
        return items.map { symbol, fallback, tooltip, action in
            GhostButton(
                symbol: symbol, fallback: fallback, caption: nil, tooltip: tooltip,
                action: action, size: actionSize, pointSize: actionPointSize,
                cornerRadius: DS.buttonRadius)
        }
    }

    /// − · slider · + · Fit. The magnifiers target the timeline directly
    /// because `EditorViewController.zoomIn(_:)` is the CANVAS zoom and the
    /// responder chain would hand them that one.
    ///
    /// Fit is not in the design's cluster, and is kept anyway: it is a real
    /// command with a menu item (⇧Z) and this button is its only one-click
    /// path.
    private static func zoomCluster(owner: TimelineView) -> NSView {
        let out = GhostButton(
            symbol: "minus.magnifyingglass", fallback: "−", caption: nil,
            tooltip: "Zoom Out", action: #selector(TimelineView.timelineZoomOut(_:)),
            size: actionSize, pointSize: 15, cornerRadius: DS.buttonRadius)
        out.target = owner
        let into = GhostButton(
            symbol: "plus.magnifyingglass", fallback: "+", caption: nil,
            tooltip: "Zoom In", action: #selector(TimelineView.timelineZoomIn(_:)),
            size: actionSize, pointSize: 15, cornerRadius: DS.buttonRadius)
        into.target = owner

        // Spec §4.4's zoom SLIDER is the shared file's `BlockSlider`
        // (`Controls.swift`, Rasterize's, kept in sync): a 3 pt track and a
        // 7 × 11 pt block knob in place of the system knob.
        let slider = owner.zoomSlider
        slider.target = owner
        slider.action = #selector(TimelineView.zoomSliderChanged(_:))
        slider.isContinuous = true
        slider.controlSize = .small
        slider.toolTip = "Timeline Zoom"
        slider.widthAnchor.constraint(equalToConstant: 110).isActive = true

        let fit = GhostButton(
            symbol: "arrow.left.and.right", fallback: "⇔", caption: nil,
            tooltip: "Fit Timeline (⇧Z)",
            action: #selector(EditorViewController.fitTimeline(_:)),
            size: actionSize, pointSize: 15, cornerRadius: DS.buttonRadius)

        let cluster = NSStackView(views: [out, slider, into, fit])
        cluster.orientation = .horizontal
        cluster.alignment = .centerY
        cluster.spacing = 8
        return cluster
    }
}

// MARK: - Zoom stepping

extension TimelineView {
    /// One click of the bar's magnifiers, as a fraction of the slider's whole
    /// travel — so a click and a drag move the zoom on the same log scale and
    /// the knob lands where the click left it.
    private static var zoomStep: CGFloat { 0.05 }

    @objc func timelineZoomOut(_ sender: Any?) { stepZoom(by: -Self.zoomStep) }
    @objc func timelineZoomIn(_ sender: Any?) { stepZoom(by: Self.zoomStep) }

    /// Anchored at the middle of what is on screen, which is where the slider
    /// anchors too: a zoom that kept time zero fixed would throw the visible
    /// edit off the right of the window.
    private func stepZoom(by delta: CGFloat) {
        let value = Self.sliderValue(for: layout.zoom) + delta
        setZoom(Self.zoom(forSlider: value), anchoredAt: content.visibleRect.midX)
    }
}

// MARK: - Selection block

/// The bar's left-edge identity block: a kind-tinted chip, the selected clip's
/// name, and the track and kind under it.
///
/// It is one of three views of one selection — the timeline's own outline and
/// the inspector's header are the others — so its chip, its word for a kind
/// and its tint all come from `ClipKindStyle` rather than being spelled again
/// here.
///
/// Its width is FIXED at the design's 176 pt minimum rather than growing with
/// the name: the point of the block is that the divider after it never moves,
/// so a long name truncates instead of pushing the tools sideways.
final class TimelineSelectionBlock: NSView {
    private var snapshot = ProjectSnapshot.empty
    private var selection: Set<UInt32> = []

    private static let width: CGFloat = 176
    private static let chip = NSSize(width: 8, height: 20)
    private static let gap: CGFloat = 8

    init() {
        super.init(frame: .zero)
    }

    @available(*, unavailable)
    required init?(coder: NSCoder) {
        fatalError("TimelineSelectionBlock does not support NSCoder")
    }

    override var isFlipped: Bool { true }

    override var intrinsicContentSize: NSSize {
        NSSize(width: Self.width, height: 30)
    }

    func update(snapshot: ProjectSnapshot, selection: Set<UInt32>) {
        self.snapshot = snapshot
        self.selection = selection
        needsDisplay = true
    }

    /// The clips the block is describing, in timeline order.
    private var clips: [ClipSnapshot] {
        snapshot.clips.filter { selection.contains($0.id) }.sorted { $0.start < $1.start }
    }

    /// Chip tint, headline and meta line for the current selection.
    ///
    /// The design covers none and one. For MANY the honest thing is the count
    /// plus whatever the clips still agree on: a shared track keeps its name
    /// and a shared kind keeps its word, and where they disagree the line says
    /// so ("3 tracks", "mixed") rather than naming one clip's and implying it
    /// holds for the rest.
    private func description() -> (tint: NSColor, name: String, meta: String) {
        let clips = self.clips
        guard let first = clips.first else {
            return (DS.borderStrong, "No selection", "")
        }
        let kinds = Set(clips.map { ClipKindStyle.name(for: $0, in: snapshot) })
        let tracks = Set(clips.map { $0.track })
        let kind = kinds.count == 1 ? ClipKindStyle.name(for: first, in: snapshot) : "mixed"
        let track =
            tracks.count == 1 ? trackName(first.track) : "\(tracks.count) tracks"
        guard clips.count > 1 else {
            return (
                ClipKindStyle.tint(for: first, in: snapshot), snapshot.name(of: first),
                "\(track) · \(kind)")
        }
        let tint = kinds.count == 1
            ? ClipKindStyle.tint(for: first, in: snapshot) : DS.borderStrong
        return (tint, "\(clips.count) clips", "\(track) · \(kind)")
    }

    /// The label a track wears in the timeline — the header column's spelling,
    /// not a second one.
    ///
    /// `set_track` accepts an EMPTY name, so `track(_:)?.name` is not enough:
    /// an unnamed track would print as nothing at all here while
    /// `TimelineHeaderView.draw` shows it as "Track 3", and the block would be
    /// naming a row the user cannot find. The number is the MODEL index + 1,
    /// which is what the header counts (0 = back), never the display row.
    private func trackName(_ id: UInt32) -> String {
        guard let index = snapshot.trackIndex(of: id) else { return "Track" }
        let name = snapshot.tracks[index].name
        return name.isEmpty ? "Track \(index + 1)" : name
    }

    override func draw(_ dirtyRect: NSRect) {
        let content = description()
        let chip = NSRect(
            x: 0, y: (bounds.height - Self.chip.height) / 2,
            width: Self.chip.width, height: Self.chip.height)
        content.tint.setFill()
        chip.fill()

        let textX = chip.maxX + Self.gap
        let textWidth = max(bounds.width - textX, 0)
        guard textWidth > 8 else { return }
        // The two lines are centred as a PAIR — 13 pt of name, 2 pt, 11 pt of
        // meta — so the block sits on the bar's centre line whatever height
        // the stack view hands it, and an empty meta line does not leave the
        // name floating high.
        let hasMeta = !content.meta.isEmpty
        let block: CGFloat = hasMeta ? 26 : 13
        let top = ((bounds.height - block) / 2).rounded()
        TimelineDraw.truncated(
            content.name,
            in: CGRect(x: textX, y: top, width: textWidth, height: 13),
            font: DS.sans(11, weight: .semibold), color: DS.textStrong)
        guard hasMeta else { return }
        TimelineDraw.truncated(
            content.meta,
            in: CGRect(x: textX, y: top + 15, width: textWidth, height: 11),
            font: DS.mono(9), color: DS.textFaint)
    }
}
